{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1976 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7b6371719bafb199401e83850c801c108d85a3f1be6aee66e1db231457326df5" :edb/db-before-t 1975 :edb/db-after-t 1976 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535288 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:19:35.033Z" 13194139535288 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341812 13194139535288 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341833 13194139535288 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341840 13194139535288 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341819 13194139535288 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341826 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341813 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1089 "q-108909" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1100 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n4.2+4.7+5.2+...=139.4\n$$" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1113 #uuid "909f6f75-e3ee-52dd-8226-a8816ba53faa" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341812 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=139.4,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=4.2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4.7-4.2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 139.4 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4.2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(8.4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(7.9+0.5N) \\\\ 278.8 & =7.9N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+7.9N-278.8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm \\sqrt{(-7.9)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-278.8)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm 24.9}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=17$ or $N=-32.8.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive and integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=17$ terms." 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341814 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341815 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341816 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341817 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341818 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1041 "selection" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341816 13194139535288 true] [17592186341813 1110 #uuid "e9dd24f7-26b9-5c21-b7c9-92d0a30568ed" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341814 1034 "33" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535288 true] [17592186341814 1111 #uuid "34539436-5aab-593a-9db3-570b029285b2" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341815 1034 "32" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535288 true] [17592186341815 1111 #uuid "4281a1aa-8a8d-520b-858f-83ce096896ca" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341816 1034 "17" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535288 true] [17592186341816 1111 #uuid "5281f2cf-1988-5c30-950a-61f4575006f9" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341817 1034 "35" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535288 true] [17592186341817 1111 #uuid "e5ecc6f9-9ab6-566b-a4f0-4642ebf61ed8" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341818 1034 "18" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535288 true] [17592186341818 1111 #uuid "c317bd27-9f07-5931-b443-7cfbc1ed0ca4" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341820 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1089 "q-108939" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1100 "An arithmetic series has the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=17,$ the $9$th term ${a}_{9}=26,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=77.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1113 #uuid "b66f3a11-061d-54d6-aa08-eb07da948258" 13194139535288 true] [17592186341819 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{6}}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{26-17}{9-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that ${a}_{6}=17.$ We can compute ${a}_{1}$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+(6-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+15 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=2,$ $d=3,$ and ${S}_{N}=77$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 77 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 3) \\\\ 154 & =N(4+3N-3) \\\\ 154 & =N(3N+1) \\\\ 154 & =3{N}^{2}+N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+N-154 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{(1)^{2}-4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-154)}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{1\\,849}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1\\pm 43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=7$ or $N=-\\frac{22}{3}.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=7$ terms." 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341821 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341822 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341823 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341824 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341825 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535288 true] [17592186341820 1041 "selec